.TH PLUGIN\-HOSTD 1 "October 2026" "plugin-hostd 0.13.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
plugin\-hostd \- supervisor of plugin host processes behind one mod\-host protocol socket
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B plugin\-hostd
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR file ]
.br
.B plugin\-hostd
.RB [ \-V | \-h ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B plugin\-hostd
speaks mod\-host's socket protocol to a controller and runs one worker process per plugin, or per named
pool of plugins, behind it: a plugin that crashes takes down its own worker and nothing else, and the
daemon puts it back. Workers are
.BR mod\-host (1)
for LV2 plugins and
.BR omx\-clap\-host (1)
for CLAP plugins, each started as
.B \-n \-p
.I port
.B \-f
.IR port ,
a feedback port of its own that the daemon reads.
Audio never passes through the daemon: every worker is a jack client named
.BI effect_ instance
as in mod\-host, and links are made in the graph.
.PP
It prints
.\" BEGIN GENERATED protocol:man-ready
.B plugin\-hostd ready!
.\" END GENERATED protocol:man-ready
once both ports accept. No worker exists until the first
.BR add .
.B quit
or SIGTERM stops every worker and the daemon.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-socket\-port =\fIport\fR
.\" BEGIN GENERATED protocol:man-port
Command port (default 5555).
.\" END GENERATED protocol:man-port
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-feedback\-port =\fIport\fR
Feedback port (default the command port plus one, unless
.B \-n
is given).
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config =\fIfile\fR
Settings file.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-nofork
Accepted; the daemon never forks.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a help text and exit.
.SH FILES
The settings live in
.IR file ,
in
.B $PLUGIN_HOSTD_CONF
or in
.IR ~/.config/plugin\-hostd.conf ,
one
.\" BEGIN GENERATED protocol:man-keys
.RI \(dq key " value" \(dq
per line: mod_host, clap_host, lv2_worker, lv2_path, state_root, pin_mode, grouping, pin_store, pin_file, pins_dir, descriptor_dir, qualification_dir, probe, history_file, host_class, ready_timeout_ms, rpc_timeout_ms, backoff_base_ms, backoff_max_ms, storm_deaths, storm_window_ms, suspect_window_ms, checkpoint_ms, idle_ms, pool_max, history_window_s, history_save_s, group_max, group_cost_max, group_rate, group_quantum, require_pins.
.\" END GENERATED protocol:man-keys
.PP
The development package installs the schemas of the documents the daemon and its probe write or read, beside
.IR protocol.json ,
in
.IR /usr/share/plugin\-hostd :
.IR protocol.schema.json ,
.IR probe\-output.schema.json
and
.IR preset.schema.json
(the org.openmixer.preset file).
.SH DIRECTORIES
What the scan of an
.B add
gathers is state, kept across a reboot, in two directories of their own:
.\" BEGIN GENERATED protocol:man-dirs
.TP
.B descriptor_dir
the scanned descriptors, one file per plugin binary (<id>@<version>\-<hash>.json, the hash the binary's SHA\-256), written by the scan of an add and read by descriptor_get; empty is $XDG_STATE_HOME/omx/descriptors, else /var/lib/omx/descriptors run as root (a system service), else $HOME/.local/state/omx/descriptors; its variable is for tests only.
The variable
.B $OMX_DESCRIPTOR_DIR
stands in for the empty default; the key in the settings file holds over it.
.TP
.B qualification_dir
plugin\-qualify's qualification records (verdict, cost, version tested), kept apart from the descriptors; empty is $XDG_STATE_HOME/omx/qualification, else /var/lib/omx/qualification run as root (a system service), else $HOME/.local/state/omx/qualification; its variable is for tests only.
The variable
.B $OMX_QUALIFICATION_DIR
stands in for the empty default; the key in the settings file holds over it.
.PP
A missing directory is made, mode 0700, with its parents. One that cannot be made or written stops the daemon before it is ready: stderr names the key and the path with resp \-528.
.PP
The answered
.B add
of a plugin whose files have no descriptor yet runs
.I probe
.B describe
in a child of its own and files its stdout as
.IR "<id>@<version>\-<hash>.json" ,
written once; the add never waits for it.
.B descriptor_get
.I uri
answers the file's path, or \-529 (none), \-530 (the scan runs), \-531 (the scan wrote nothing).
Once the descriptor is in place the same scan runs
.I probe
.B panel
and files the plugin's panel as
.IR "panels/<id>@<version>\-<hash>.json" ,
under the descriptor's name;
.B panel_get
.I uri
answers its path, with the same codes.
.\" END GENERATED protocol:man-dirs
.SH PINS
With require_pins on,
.B add
admits only a plugin that has a pin.
With pin_mode strict the pin comes from
.B pin_set
or from the
.B pin_file
loaded at start; with pin_mode first\-use, the default, the first
.B add
of a plugin with no pin records it in the pin store and places the instance own.
A change after that is refused and announced by the
.B pin_changed
event until
.B pin_accept
takes the new pin.
.PP
Packages ship pins in a read\-only layer: every
.I *.pins
file of
.B pins_dir
(by default
.IR /usr/share/plugin\-hostd/pins.d )
is read at start, in name order, in the same line format.
A pin there with verdict=refused always holds: neither the pin store, the
.B pin_file
nor
.B pin_set
overrides it, and
.B pin_set
and
.B pin_accept
of its uri answer \-516.
Any other package pin is under the host's: a pin of the same uri in the store, the
.B pin_file
or from
.B pin_set
holds over it, and
.B pin_clear
lets the package pin hold again.
When two files hold the same uri a refusal wins, otherwise the last file by name.
A bad line is skipped and named on stderr; a missing or empty directory holds no pins.
.PP
What cannot be pinned:
.\" BEGIN GENERATED protocol:man-pin-limits
.IP \(bu 2
a path holding ',', '=' or whitespace cannot be pinned: they are the separators of pin_set's words, and pin_set refuses it as a token outside its grammar.
.IP \(bu 2
a pin_set line is at most line_max, 4096 bytes, the protocol socket's buffer; a longer one is refused as outside the grammar.
.IP \(bu 2
a manifest that uses @base, or names a file of the plugin by a percent\-encoded IRI, makes add answer PHD_ERR_PIN_BINARY_MISMATCH (\-509): the daemon does not resolve either, so it cannot know the file the host would load.
.\" END GENERATED protocol:man-pin-limits
.SH WORKER FEEDBACK
What a worker writes on its feedback port reaches the controller on the daemon's:
.\" BEGIN GENERATED protocol:man-relay
.IP \(bu 2
every line a worker writes on its feedback port goes out on the daemon's feedback port as the worker wrote it: output_set, param_set, data_finish and the rest of mod\-host's feedback, and a line the daemon does not know.
.IP \(bu 2
the instance number in a relayed line is already the controller's: add hands it to the worker unchanged, so nothing in a line is rewritten.
.IP \(bu 2
a worker's lines go out in the order it wrote them, and what a worker wrote before it died goes out before its worker_died event; an instance lives in one worker at a time, so its lines keep their order across a respawn; the lines of different workers interleave.
.IP \(bu 2
monitor_output is in the ledger, one line per output, and param_monitor as it was sent; a respawn replays them after the add, so a respawned instance reports the same outputs, each once, starting again from its first value.
.IP \(bu 2
output_data_ready names no instance and goes to every worker; each worker's data_finish is relayed.
.IP \(bu 2
with no feedback port, or no controller on it, a line is dropped, as mod\-host drops it; the daemon reads every worker's feedback all the time, so no worker waits on it.
.\" END GENERATED protocol:man-relay
.SH HOST SETTINGS
What the daemon keeps of the commands every worker shares, so that a synced plugin comes back at the right tempo after a crash:
.\" BEGIN GENERATED protocol:man-host-settings
.IP \(bu 2
the tempo, the metre and the transport a controller sets (mod\-host's set_bpm, set_bpb, transport and transport_sync) are kept, the latest line of each as it was sent, in the order they were last sent.
.IP \(bu 2
a worker is handed them before anything else: a respawned one before its first add, and a worker started later, of a new placement, as well; a worker's refusal of one is written to stderr and is no failure.
.IP \(bu 2
a line whose words are not that command's grammar is passed on and not kept.
.\" END GENERATED protocol:man-host-settings
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mod\-host (1),
.BR omx\-clap\-host (1)
.PP
The protocol, the placement of an instance, the verbs the daemon adds and the events on the feedback port are
declared in
.I plugin\-hostd/protocol.h
and described in the README of the source tree.
